---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import FeatureFigures from '../../components/FeatureFigures.astro';
import { article, breadcrumbs } from '../../lib/site/jsonld';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

const PATH = '/guides/share-a-markdown-document-with-a-password/';
const DESC = 'Send a Markdown document as a link that only opens with a password. Encrypted on your device, no server, no account. How it works, why expiry is soft, and its limits.';
const HEADLINE = 'Share a Markdown document with a password';
---
<Page title={HEADLINE} description={DESC} path={PATH} current="guides" ogType="article" ogImage="/og-share-password.png"
  jsonLd={[article({ path: PATH, site: Astro.site, headline: HEADLINE, description: DESC, published: '2026-11-17', modified: '2026-11-17' }), breadcrumbs([{ name: 'mdprev', path: '/' }, { name: 'Guides', path: '/guides/' }, { name: HEADLINE, path: PATH }], Astro.site)]}>
  <article class="art gd">
    <span class="st-lbl">Guide</span>
    <h1>{HEADLINE}</h1>
    <p class="by">Updated 17 November 2026. Describes what the <a href={url('/app/')}>mdprev editor</a> does today; the numbers come from its built-in sample document.</p>

    <div class="callout"><b>SHORT ANSWER</b><p>In the <a href={url('/app/')}>editor</a>, choose <b>Export → Share with password…</b>, type a password of at least 8 characters and press <b>Copy link</b>. The document is encrypted on your device and carried inside the link; the password is not. Send the password another way. An expiry date is optional and soft: anyone who already opened or copied the text keeps it.</p></div>

    <p>A normal mdprev share link (<code>#d=</code>) carries the compressed document itself, so anyone who has the link can read it. A password link (<code>#e=</code>) carries the same document encrypted. Without the password the link is unreadable text, and there is no copy on a server to leak, because no server ever receives it.</p>

    <FeatureFigures name="share" class="gd-fig" />

    <h2>1. Make the link</h2>
    <p>Open your document in the <a href={url('/app/')}>editor</a>. In the <b>Export</b> menu (in the <b>File</b> menu on a phone), choose <b>Share with password…</b>. Type the password, pick an expiry if you want one (No expiry, 1 day, 7 days or 30 days; expiry is soft, see below) and press <b>Copy link</b>. The link is now on your clipboard.</p>
    <p>Passwords shorter than 8 characters are refused. The encrypted link can be copied by anyone it passes through, and they can try passwords on it as often as they like on their own computer, so a long password that is not used elsewhere matters more here than on a website that limits attempts.</p>

    <h2>2. Send the link and the password separately</h2>
    <p>Paste the link in one place (an email, a chat) and give the password another way, for example by voice or in a different app. Whoever has both can read the document; whoever has only one cannot.</p>

    <h2>3. What the reader sees</h2>
    <p>Opening the link shows a password box before anything else. The right password opens the document in their editor, replacing what was on their screen; their own text stays in <b>Restore previous</b>. A wrong password and a damaged link get the same message, so the page gives away nothing about which it was. If the link has expired by the reader’s clock, it is refused before the password is asked, and the page shows the date it expired. That is a soft limit, explained below.</p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The document is compressed, then encrypted with <b>AES-GCM</b> in your browser, using the Web Crypto API that browsers provide.</li>
      <li>The key comes from your password through <b>PBKDF2-SHA256 with 600,000 rounds</b> and a random salt, so each guess costs an attacker real work.</li>
      <li>The rounds, salt and expiry date sit in a short header that is bound to the encrypted text. Changing any of them, the expiry included, makes the link fail to open. The expiry itself is still soft: it is checked on the reader’s clock.</li>
      <li>The link keeps everything after the <code>#</code>. Browsers do not send that part to the web server when the page loads, so the server only sees a request for the editor page.</li>
      <li>The password is never put in the link, in the browser’s storage or in a log.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Encryption needs an HTTPS page and a recent browser. On a plain <code>http://</code> page the option says so instead of making the link.</p>

    <h2>Expiry is soft</h2>
    <p>An expiry date is checked by the reader’s own browser, against the reader’s own clock. After that date, mdprev refuses to open the link. It cannot pull back a copy: anyone who opened the document before then or copied the text still has it, and someone with the link and the password can set their computer’s clock back. Use expiry to keep a link from being opened by accident later, not to take back something you sent.</p>

    <h2>How long the link gets</h2>
    <table class="st-table">
      <caption>The built-in sample document (60 lines, 1,495 characters), measured 17 November 2026. Counts are the characters after the prefix.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Link</th><th scope="col" class="n">Characters</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>#d=</code>, no password</td><td class="n">1,066</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>#e=</code>, with a password</td><td class="n">1,164</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>#e=</code>, with a password and a (soft) expiry</td><td class="n">1,170</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The password adds about 100 characters, whatever the size of the document. Links longer than 16,000 characters are not made at all: for a long document, export a file instead. Some chat apps cut long links, so test one before you rely on it.</p>

    <h2>Limits, plainly</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The link is the document. Anyone with the link and the password can read it, and keep it.</li>
      <li>Expiry is soft, as above. There is no way to revoke a link once sent.</li>
      <li>There is no account and no record of who opened a link.</li>
      <li>A document theme travels inside the encrypted link. Custom CSS never does.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <p><a href={url('/features/')}>Everything free in mdprev, compared with paid plans</a>. To put many notes online instead, <a href={url('/guides/publish-a-markdown-vault-as-a-static-site/')}>publish a vault as a static site</a>. The <a href={url('/privacy/')}>privacy page</a> covers share links in general.</p>
  </article>
</Page>
<style>
  .gd :global(.gd-fig) { margin: 6px 0 26px; max-width: 520px; }
</style>
